# The dependencies this app actually asks for. Edit THIS file, never
# requirements.txt — that one is generated from this one and holds the whole
# resolved tree.
#
#   uv pip compile --universal --python-version 3.11 \
#       --output-file requirements.txt requirements.in
#
# Run it after every edit here and commit both files together; CI regenerates
# it and fails if the two disagree, so a forgotten run shows up as a red
# build rather than as two machines running different code.
#
# Use the uv named below. The check compares the generated file byte for byte,
# so a different uv can reorder a line or reword the header and turn a correct
# change red. The workflow reads this line rather than repeating the number, so
# bumping it here is the whole change — then recompile and commit both files.
#
# uv-version: 0.10.9
#
# Recompiling in this directory keeps the existing requirements.txt as pinned
# preferences, which is what you want: it re-resolves what your edit changed
# and leaves the other 42 packages where they were. Deliberately upgrading
# everything is `-U`, and it is a separate commit with its own test run.
#
# --universal resolves for every platform at once and writes the environment
# markers, because the checkouts are macOS and the container and CI are Linux.
# Compiling on one of them alone drops the other's platform-specific packages —
# the Linux install pulls SecretStorage and jeepney, which a macOS resolve
# never sees.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, in one measurement: it names 5 packages, and a
# clean install puts 47 in the venv. Pinning by hand pins the 5. On 2026-09-01
# the app broke because one of the other 42 changed a major version overnight.
#
# Not using --generate-hashes yet. It would add integrity checking on every
# container start, which is worth having; it also makes any hash mismatch a
# hard install failure, and init.sh continues startup after a failed install.
# Decide that separately from this file's job, which is reproducibility.

streamlit==1.52.0
# Pinned, not a floor. `>=2.11.0` resolved to 2.35.3 on a fresh install
# (2026-08-28) and the confirmation gate stops working there: a destructive
# tool the model asks for is neither executed NOR raised for approval — the
# run just continues and answers as if it were done. Nothing is deleted, so
# it is not a safety hole, but the user is told a thing happened that did
# not. Every checkout in use is on 2.15.0, where all 13 agent_runtime tests
# pass; the floor meant developers and a fresh install ran different code.
# Raise this deliberately, with test_a_deferred_destructive_tool_is_still_gated
# green, not by letting the resolver choose.
pydantic-ai-slim[openai,mcp]==2.15.0
# Pinning 2.15.0 was not enough, because its own MCP dependency is a floor:
# `pydantic-ai-slim[mcp]` asks for `fastmcp-slim[client]>=3.3.0`, and on
# 2026-09-01 that resolved to 4.0.0, which moved from `mcp<2.0` to
# `mcp>=2.0`. mcp 2.x renamed the capability fields to snake_case
# (`PromptsCapability.listChanged` → `list_changed`) while
# pydantic_ai/mcp.py:578 still reads the old spelling, so every chat turn
# died at MCP handshake time with
#   AttributeError: 'PromptsCapability' object has no attribute 'listChanged'
# Nothing in the app was wrong; the deployed container had simply been
# installed on a day the resolver answered differently.
#
# Three lines, because they say different things. `fastmcp-slim` names the
# package that actually moved — 3.4.7 is the newest 3.x, is what the deployed
# slot runs, and was installed into a clean venv here with the whole suite
# green. `mcp<2.0` names the invariant that made the move fatal: this
# pydantic-ai reads the v1 spelling. It stays even though the `==` below
# implies it, because the exact version is a fact about today and the ceiling
# is the reason it was chosen. The pin is exact rather than a bare `<2.0`,
# because a ceiling alone still lets a fresh install land anywhere in
# 1.24–1.x while the checkouts sit on one version — the same "developers and
# a fresh install ran different code" this file was already written to stop,
# eight lines up. Raise any of them deliberately, with
# test_the_installed_mcp_sdk_matches_what_pydantic_ai_reads green.
fastmcp-slim[client]==3.4.7
mcp==1.29.1  # invariant: must stay <2.0 for this pydantic-ai
# Same shape again, one layer down: `>=2.0.0` let a fresh install take openai
# 3.6.0 while every developer checkout ran 2.46.0. pydantic-ai 2.15.0 declares
# `openai>=2.45.0` with no ceiling, and a missing ceiling is not an
# endorsement of the next major — 3.x shipped after it. Held at the 2.46.0 the
# checkouts run, which is inside the range pydantic-ai actually declares. The
# unit tests pass on 3.6.0 too, but they drive a FunctionModel and never make
# a real API call, so they are not evidence about this pin either way.
openai==2.46.0
